  "articleBody": "Removing an old shower stall often starts as a cosmetic update, but it quickly involves supply plumbing, drain stacks, and concealed framing. Knowing when to step back and bring in an experienced contractor protects your home from expensive structural damage.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\n- Hidden water damage: If removing the shower pan reveals dark, spongy subflooring, mold growth, or soft wall studs, you need professional framing repairs and proper drying before proceeding.\n- Plumbing relocation or line capping: Adjusting water supply lines, moving a drain, or capping lines inside a shared wall requires proper pipe sizing, soldering, or approved mechanical fittings to prevent concealed leaks.\n- Outdated or regulated materials: Homes with vintage adhesives, plaster, or drywall textures may contain hazardous substances that require specialized handling or certified abatement specialists.\n- Complex waterproofing integration: Transitioning from an old acrylic enclosure to custom tile requires proper cement board, membrane barriers, and pre-slope pan construction to keep framing dry long-term.\n- Electrical proximity: Moving walls or fixtures near existing GFCI circuits or bathroom wiring requires careful trade coordination to avoid safety hazards.\n\nTaking out a shower involves far more than swinging a hammer. For expert shower removal, structural repairs, and tile preparation across the Denver metro area, call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}